+Assume full AC. Let $K\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot, $E_K$ its exterior, and $\rho:\pi_1(E_K)\to SL_2(\mathbb C)$ a representation. The **adjoint local system** $\operatorname{Ad}\rho$ has fibre $\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$ and monodromy $v\mapsto\rho(\gamma)v\rho(\gamma)^{-1}$. Its twisted homology $H_*(E_K;\operatorname{Ad}\rho)$ is the intrinsic local-system homology with this monodromy, equivalently the homology of the cellular local-coefficient complex for a supplied CW structure; the same convention defines boundary-torus twisted homology and the inclusion map $i_*:H_1(\partial E_K;\operatorname{Ad}\rho)\to H_1(E_K;\operatorname{Ad}\rho)$. An **admissible adjoint representation** is one for which there is a nonzero vector $v_\rho\in\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)$ fixed by the peripheral adjoint action such that $\{[v_\rho\otimes\lambda],[v_\rho\otimes\mu]\}$ is a basis of $H_1(\partial E_K;\operatorname{Ad}\rho)$ and $\ker i_*$ is one-dimensional. For an irreducible affine character curve $X$, its function field $\mathbb C(X)$ is the fraction field of its coordinate domain. A **tautological representation** over $\mathbb C(X)$ is a homomorphism $\rho_X:\pi_1(E_K)\to SL_2(\mathbb C(X))$ whose character is the generic character of $X$. A **smooth projective completion** is a smooth complete curve birational to $X$ that contains $X$ as a dense open subset. An **ideal point** is a point $x$ of that completion outside $X$; it defines the discrete valuation $v_x(f)=\operatorname{ord}_x(f)$ on $\mathbb C(X)$. The valuation ring is $\mathcal O_x=\{f:v_x(f)\ge0\}$, and a **uniformizer** is an element $\pi\in\mathcal O_x$ with $v_x(\pi)=1$. Its residue field is $k_x=\mathcal O_x/(\pi)$. An $\mathcal O_x$-lattice in $\mathbb C(X)^2$ is a free rank-two $\mathcal O_x$-submodule spanning $\mathbb C(X)^2$; two lattices are homothetic if one is a nonzero scalar multiple of the other. The **Bruhat–Tits tree** has homothety classes of these lattices as vertices, with two vertices adjacent if representatives satisfy $\pi\Lambda\subsetneq\Lambda'\subsetneq\Lambda$ and $\Lambda/\Lambda'$ is one-dimensional over $k_x$. Whenever the tautological representation exists, it acts on this tree. The ideal point, valuation, action, and any essential surface produced from the action are its Culler–Shalen data; that surface is called the associated Culler–Shalen surface.
 
